A||||9||Lurch||88 v-6 swap||||6||
Z||000000||Lurch||05-01-2000||10:46 PM||||Looking for info on swapping 2.8/3.1 into 88 coupe.  Heres what I have:<BR>1.  88 coupe, 2.5, Izuzu 5spd, previous owner took ecm, throttle cable, throttle body to repair his nephew's car.<BR>2.  85 gt, 2.8, auto trans, wiring harnesses under the engine cover are complete and I have ecm for this car. Engine and accessories are complete and unmolested.<BR>3.  89 3.1, Gen II, with aluminum heads, complete.  One head leaks water to the outside (not into a cylinder}.<P>This is what I plan to do:<P>1.  rebuild 3.1 short block using correct pistons for 2.8 iron heads.<BR>2.  recondition iron 2.8 heads and install<BR>fiero top end on 3.1.<P>My questions to the members of the forum are these:<P>1.  Will the 85 ecm and prom chip work with the rest of the 88 car?<BR>2.  If not, what is the simplest and most available ecm, chip, harness combo that will work?<P>I have read previous posts about similar swaps where the under dash harness was swapped also, but the reasons for this were not entirely clear.  Thanks in advance for any information and experience you may be able to share on this subject.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Raydar||05-02-2000||04:07 PM||||Are you gonna keep the Isuzu 5-speed? (I would.) ||||reg||1
Z||000002||dman||05-02-2000||05:19 PM||||Hello Lurch. I too will be peforming a similar swap. I have an 87 4 cylinder 5 speed Isuzu target and an 85 GT 4 speed Muncie as the source. I would like to perform a straight swap. But if the v6 is in rough shape I may get a 3.4 pushrod engine instead (but use the 2.8 upper and accessories).<P>I believe at least one forum regular has done something similar. I think it was "SCCA Fiero" and he has the following URL available that discusses his conversion:<BR> <A HREF="http://msnhomepages.talkcity.com/RallyRd/scca_fiero/4cylindertoV6.html" TARGET=_blank>http://msnhomepages.talkcity.com/RallyRd/scca_fiero/4cylindertoV6.html</A> <P>I am also confused about the necessity of changing some of the electrical. I know the engine wiring harness needs to be swapped (just the harness that resides inside of the engine compartment --- that mates with the connector on the firewall). I have also read somewhere that additional rewiring is sometimes needed; this is something I don't quite understand.<P>I believe SCCA Fiero had an 88 target, the same as you. In fact his web page mentions a few differences in the cradle design of the 88 that he had to deal with.<P>Since I haven't performed my conversion yet I don't have any answers for you. I hope though that you will document and share some of the information that you obtain during your conversion.<P>Good luck with your conversion and have fun!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||TheFiero64||05-02-2000||06:12 PM||||I did the same swap about 10 months ago. The car has a lot more power. I used the engine out of an 86 SE.  It basically bolted right on.  I just had to drill new holes for the front motor mount and the torque strut.  The wiring was too easy.  It plugs right in.  You just remove the 4 cyl. computer and put the V6 one in and plug the harness already<BR>attached to the engine into it and into the block next to the battery.  I didn't change any other wires and everything runs great.  The only problem is the darn fuel pump.  You must change it because of the pressure<BR>difference.  The fuel lines are already lined up. The coolant hoses are a bit tricky to negotiate, you must cut a couple of inches off of the tube that runs along the back of the engine to clear the 88 Suspension, but if you have an 88 motor you don't need to trim it. It was a two day swap and a lot of fun. I would do it again if I had another 4 cyl 88. It was worth it.<BR>Good Luck on your swap. My only problem now is I just found out the engine was from an 88 and the flywheel is an 86 so I have to pull it out and replace the flywheel <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>P.S. Use the isuzu 5 speed I love mine!<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||05-02-2000||09:26 PM||||Fiero64, you didn't have to replace the rear wiring harness for the tail lights? I left the Coupe harness in and when I hit the brakes the interior lights would come on along with the radiator fan. I swapped it for the 86SE harness and it was fine. I agree 10000.99%... the fuel pump thing sucked. <P>As Fiero64 stated the complete wiring harness from the fuse box to junction box has to be swapped... don't forget the throttle cable. I also had to cut about 2 inches off of the metal cooling pipe to clear the suspension. The motor mount won't line up either... I think it needs to be moved back 1 3/4"  because the 88 mount is relocated to make room for the laterial links. But the fuel lines were a piece-o-cake.<P>P.S. The Isuzu is slick... use it, but if you want a Getrag I may be talked out of the one sitting in my shed for the right price  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Lurch||05-02-2000||10:24 PM||||Thanks for the replies!<BR>Yes, I plan on leaving the Izuzu in the car.<BR>Fiero64, was your swap 85 v-6 auto to 88 5spd ?  I would just like to confirm what ecm you used in the 88.<P>Right now I'm accumulating the parts and repairing some of the collateral damage to the 88.  It was the subject of some abuse during a domestic disagreement.  Irate boyfriend kicked a hole in the left doorskin and broke the window glass, broke the windshield, and left headlight door.  The guy I bought the car from had loaded it on a trailer with a bobcat and got the left front fender and nose.  This all sounds pretty bad but I gave 50 bucks for this car, with mags, 50 bucks for the 85 gt with engine, and 40 for a complete non running 84 with mags and good windshield.  So, I think I have most of the parts necessary to put the 88 together.<BR>  Speaking of Getrags, saturday I was down to the local pull-it-yourself yard looking for a few odds and ends and found a Getrag in the core pile. It had the extension housing on the right side like a Berretta{?}.  They wanted 35$ for it.  Might go back and get it in case the Izuzu is no good.<BR>  By the way, did you guys use a v-6 clutch and the Izuzu release bearing?<BR>  I will keep track of what I use and post it on the forum.  Hate to get fast and loose with a buck on these projects-<BR>  Thanks again for the replies.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||05-02-2000||11:23 PM||||NO NO NO NO!!<P>I don't know how that started about useing the V6 clutch and the Isuzu T.O. bearing. I followed the same advise and ended up dropping the cradle <B>again</B> because the car wouldn't move. Use the clutch for the tranny not the motor... the motor has nothing to do with it. Believe me, you don't wanna go through what I went through. Learn from my mistakes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||05-02-2000||11:26 PM||||As far as the ECM goes, I took my motor out of an 86SE and, of course, I used the ECM from the 86SE. There is only one problem with this and that is the shift light. No big deal... it just doesn't come on to tell you to shift to 5th because it doesn't know it's there. Oh yeah... my 86SE was a 4 speed manual.||||reg||1
Z||000008||TheFiero64||05-03-2000||11:52 AM||||I had the same thing a 86SE with a 4 speed. I left the clutch from the 86 on the flywheel and used a new TO bearing for the isuzu trans.||||reg||1
Z||000009||SCCA FIERO||05-03-2000||08:03 PM||||Huh? You used the V6 clutch and the Isuzu T.O. bearing?||||reg||1
